ist contains repeated values, the result should also be the same. Considering equivariance under shifting and non-negative scaling. Also, if the input data is already sorted, then the median can be directly obtained without further sorting. The latter seems like a potentially significant optimization.\n\n\nThe sorted list optimization, based on `already_sorted_thm`, offers substantial efficiency gains. The proof, leveraging `unified_index_thm` and `mergeSort_eq_self_of_sorted`, is concise. Also, considering generalization to `List Rat` or `List Real` seems appropriate, given the mathematical context.\n\n\nThe Python implementation consistently uses integer inputs for the `median_low` function and its test cases, which may have implications for the mathematical formalization. Considering potential domain restrictions, checking whether the theorem instantiation should be restricted to integers, or if generalization to other numeric types such as rationals or reals is sound. Focusing on verifying the consistency between the implementation, test cases, and the formalized properties.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"edit_file","args":{"Path":"/home/user/lean_project/Veribench/28_statistics_median_low.lean","Replacement":"/-- Property 15: Translation equivariance: shifting every element by `c` shifts the low median by `c`. -/\n@[reducible]\ndef shift_equivariant_prop (data : List Int) (c : Int) : Prop :=\n  benchmark_median_low (data.map (· + c)) = (benchmark_median_low data).map (· + c)\n\ntheorem shift_equivariant_thm (data : List Int) (c : Int) :\n    shift_equivariant_prop data c := by\n  unfold shift_equivariant_prop\n  rw [benchmark_eq_median_low, benchmark_eq_median_low]\n  unfold median_low\n  dsimp only\n  have hmono : Monotone (fun x : Int => x + c) := fun a b hab => by dsimp; omega\n  rw [mergeSort_map_of_monotone data (· + c) hmono]\n  simp only [List.length_map]\n  split_ifs with h0 hodd\n  · rfl\n  · exact List.getElem?_map\n  · exact List.getElem?_map\n\n/-- P